# {{ name }}

A small command line tool for {{ display_name }}, generated by formwork_engine {{ foundation_version }}.
It turns a CSV or JSON table into one branded HTML report that opens offline: a masthead with the
firm wordmark, a metric strip (rows, columns), the table, status pills, and a footer with the support
link. These files are managed: regenerate the workspace instead of editing them. Put your own
modules in new files beside them.

It needs only CPython 3.10 or newer and the standard library. There is nothing to install.

## Run it

From this folder (`apps/{{ name }}`), with the `src` folder on the import path:

```powershell
$env:PYTHONPATH = "src"
py -3 -m {{ package }} report sample.csv --output report.html
```

```sh
PYTHONPATH=src python3 -m {{ package }} report sample.csv --output report.html
```

Then open `report.html` in a browser. It is a single portable file: the stylesheet is embedded and the
brand SVGs are embedded as data URIs when they are under 256 KB (larger assets are copied to an
`assets/` folder beside the report instead). It loads nothing from the network.

Options:

- `INPUT` is a `.csv` file (header row first, UTF-8) or a `.json` file holding a list of objects.
- `--output OUT.html` (required) is the file to write.
- `--title T` sets the heading. The default is the input file name.
- `--status-column C` turns that column's values into status pills. The default is a column named
  `Status`, if there is one. Recognized words get a tone (`Issued` is success, `In review` is warning,
  `Blocked` is danger); anything else is neutral.

Every value from the input is HTML-escaped, so a cell that contains `<script>` is shown as text.

## Optional: install it

`pip install -e .` registers a `{{ name }}` command (`{{ name }} report sample.csv --output report.html`)
and takes the `PYTHONPATH` step away. It is not required, and it builds nothing beyond the
standard-library setuptools backend declared in `pyproject.toml`.

## Test it

```powershell
$env:PYTHONPATH = "src"
py -3 -m unittest discover -s tests
```

## Look and identity

`src/{{ package }}/theme.css` is generated from the firm's design tokens and component choices,
the same source as the workspace's HTML guide. The report reads it at run time, so it never drifts
from what is embedded. Packaged fonts are not copied here: a report uses the token font stack, which
falls back to system fonts, and shows a brand font only where it is installed.

The package and command names (`{{ package }}`, `{{ name }}`) come from `technical.namespace` and never change when
the firm is rebranded. The display name, logo text and support link in reports follow `identity` in
the firm configuration.
